"""Form 5 Food and Nutrition: nutrients, digestion, the kitchen, equipment and hygiene (11 lessons, one practice set each)."""

from .dsl import *  # noqa: F403

COURSE = ("food-and-nutrition", "form-5")

M1 = "Basic Nutrition & Digestion"
M2 = "The Kitchen, Equipment & Hygiene"

LESSONS = []

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 1. Proteins
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Proteins",
    title="Proteins: building and repairing the body",
    slug="fdn1-proteins",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Proteins are made of amino acids. They build and repair the body, come from animal and plant foods, and a lack of protein causes kwashiorkor and marasmus.",
    objectives=[
        "Define protein, amino acid and essential amino acid",
        "Name animal and plant sources of protein, including Cameroonian foods",
        "State the functions of protein in the body",
        "Describe kwashiorkor and marasmus and explain how to combine plant proteins",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Protein", "Amino acid", "Essential amino acid", "High biological value (HBV)", "Kwashiorkor", "Marasmus", "Protein complementation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A child in a village near Bafoussam grows from a baby of 3 kg to an adult of 60 kg. A farmer's cut hand closes up and heals. "
          "Both of these need the same nutrient: **protein**. Without enough protein a child cannot grow well and a wound heals slowly."),
        T("A **nutrient** is a substance in food that the body uses for energy, growth, repair or protection. Protein is one of the "
          "main nutrients. In this lesson you will learn what protein is made of, where to find it in our local foods, what it does and "
          "what happens when a person does not get enough."),
        D("**Proteins** are large nutrient molecules made of smaller units called **amino acids** joined in long chains. Proteins contain "
          "carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and **nitrogen** (some also contain sulphur).", title="Definition: protein"),
        T("Nitrogen is what makes protein special. Carbohydrates and fats do not contain nitrogen. There are about twenty different amino acids. "
          "The body can make some of them. Others it cannot make, so they must come from food."),
        D("An **essential amino acid** is an amino acid that the body cannot make for itself, so it must be supplied by the food we eat.",
          title="Definition: essential amino acid", note="Adults need eight essential amino acids; infants need one or two more. Check your textbook for the list it uses."),
        H("Kinds of protein and food sources"),
        T("Foods are often grouped by the quality of their protein. **High biological value (HBV)** protein contains all the essential amino acids "
          "in good amounts. **Low biological value (LBV)** protein is short of one or more of them."),
        KEY("Two groups of protein foods", "Most animal proteins are HBV. Most plant proteins are LBV. Soya beans are a plant food with protein of unusually good quality.", cards=[
            ["Animal sources (mostly HBV)", "Fish, meat, chicken, eggs, milk, cheese, snails, crayfish, bush meat."],
            ["Plant sources (mostly LBV)", "Beans, cowpeas, groundnuts, bambara nuts, soya, egusi (melon seeds), nuts, maize, and leaves such as eru in smaller amounts."],
        ], note="Eru is valued for its protein among green leaves, but a bowl of eru alone is not a rich source of protein: it is usually eaten with fish or meat."),
        T("**Protein complementation** means eating two plant foods together so that each supplies the amino acids the other lacks. "
          "Beans eaten with maize (as in corn and beans), or groundnuts with a cereal, give a better mixture than either food alone. "
          "This is useful for families who cannot often afford meat or fish."),
        H("Functions of protein"),
        KEY("What protein does in the body", "", cards=[
            ["Growth", "Builds new cells in children, adolescents and pregnant women."],
            ["Repair", "Replaces worn cells and heals wounds, burns and broken bones."],
            ["Body chemicals", "Makes enzymes (which speed up digestion and other reactions), some hormones, and antibodies that fight disease."],
            ["Energy", "If carbohydrate and fat are short, protein is burned for energy. It gives about 17 kJ per gram."],
        ], note="Using protein for energy is wasteful: its best job is growth and repair."),
        EX("Worked example 1: sources and functions", intro="Name three animal sources and three plant sources of protein found in Cameroon. State two functions of protein.", steps=[
            S("Animal sources", text="Fish (for example mackerel, 'titus'), eggs and beef."),
            S("Plant sources", text="Beans, groundnuts and soya."),
            S("Function 1", text="Growth: it builds new body cells in children."),
            S("Function 2", text="Repair: it replaces worn cells and heals wounds."),
        ]),
        H("Deficiency diseases"),
        T("When the diet has too little protein, children suffer most because they are growing. There are two main diseases of protein-energy malnutrition."),
        D("**Kwashiorkor** is a disease of young children whose diet is very low in protein, often after weaning from breast milk onto a diet of mostly starchy food. "
          "Signs: swollen feet and face (oedema), a swollen stomach, thin and pale hair, sore skin, tiredness and a sad, miserable look.", title="Definition: kwashiorkor"),
        D("**Marasmus** is caused by a severe lack of food overall: too little energy as well as too little protein. "
          "Signs: a very thin body with the ribs showing, wasted muscles, loose skin and an old, wrinkled face. There is no swelling.", title="Definition: marasmus"),
        TIP("To remember the difference: kwashiorkor = swollen (oedema), marasmus = wasted (very thin). A child with kwashiorkor can look round and puffy even though "
            "the child is starving of protein."),
        MISTAKE("Saying protein is the main source of energy", "The body's main fuel is carbohydrate, with fat also giving energy. Protein can give energy, but its main jobs are growth and repair."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up the two diseases", "Kwashiorkor has oedema (swelling) and mainly lacks protein. Marasmus is wasting from too little of everything. Do not write that kwashiorkor is 'very thin'."),
        EX("Worked example 2: planning a cheap protein meal", intro="A family cannot buy meat. Suggest a meal that gives good protein and explain why it works.", steps=[
            S("Choose two plant foods", text="Beans and maize (or beans and rice, or groundnuts and cassava)."),
            S("Explain complementation", text="Each food is short of different amino acids, so together they supply a better mixture."),
            S("Add a small animal food", text="A little dried fish or an egg makes the protein even better."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which food gives the best-quality (HBV) protein?", ["Cassava", "Egg", "Ripe plantain", "Palm oil"], 1,
             "An egg contains all the essential amino acids in good amounts.", hint="Think of animal foods.",
             feedback=["Cassava is mostly starch and very low in protein.", "", "Plantain is mainly carbohydrate.", "Palm oil is a fat."]),
        QUIZ("A young child has a swollen stomach, swollen feet and thin pale hair. Which disease is likely?", ["Marasmus", "Scurvy", "Kwashiorkor", "Goitre"], 2,
             "Swelling (oedema) with poor hair and skin points to kwashiorkor, a protein deficiency.", hint="Which disease has oedema?",
             feedback=["Marasmus gives severe wasting, not swelling.", "Scurvy is a vitamin C deficiency with bleeding gums.", "", "Goitre is a swelling of the neck from lack of iodine."]),
        TIP("Exam link: protein sources, functions and kwashiorkor often appear in Paper 1 multiple choice. In Paper 2, questions on nutrients usually ask you to name sources, "
            "state functions and describe the deficiency disease. Give Cameroonian examples when the question allows."),
        PQ("(a) Define the term protein. (b) State three functions of protein.",
           "(a) A protein is a nutrient made of amino acids joined together; it contains carbon, hydrogen, oxygen and nitrogen. "
           "(b) Growth of new cells; repair of worn or damaged cells; making enzymes, hormones and antibodies; (energy when other sources are short)."),
        PQ("Give four good sources of protein in Cameroon, two animal and two plant.",
           "Animal: fish, eggs, meat, milk. Plant: beans, groundnuts, soya, cowpeas, egusi."),
        PQ("Describe two signs of kwashiorkor and say what causes it.",
           "Signs: swollen feet and face (oedema), swollen stomach, thin pale hair, sore skin, tiredness. It is caused by a diet that is very low in protein."),
        PQ("Explain why a mixture of beans and maize is better than maize alone.",
           "Maize alone is short of some essential amino acids. Beans supply those amino acids, and maize supplies others that beans lack, so the mixture gives better-quality protein (complementation)."),
        SUM("Protein is made of amino acids and contains nitrogen.",
            "Animal proteins (fish, eggs, meat, milk) are mostly high biological value; plant proteins (beans, groundnuts, soya) are mostly lower and are best combined.",
            "Functions: growth, repair, enzymes, hormones and antibodies, and energy (about 17 kJ per gram) when needed.",
            "Kwashiorkor (swelling, lack of protein) and marasmus (severe wasting, lack of food overall) are the deficiency diseases."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 2. Carbohydrates
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Carbohydrates",
    title="Carbohydrates: sugars, starch and energy",
    slug="fdn1-carbohydrates",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Carbohydrates are sugars, starch and cellulose. They are the body's main source of energy, and cassava, plantain, rice and maize are our staple sources.",
    objectives=[
        "Classify carbohydrates into sugars, starch and cellulose",
        "Name food sources, including local staples",
        "State the functions of carbohydrates and the effects of too much or too little",
        "Describe simple tests for starch and sugar",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Carbohydrate", "Monosaccharide", "Disaccharide", "Polysaccharide", "Starch", "Glucose", "Staple food", "Obesity"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In most Cameroonian homes the biggest part of the plate is a carbohydrate food: fufu made from cassava or cocoyam, boiled or fried plantain, rice, maize or yam. "
          "These are **staple foods**: foods eaten in large amounts that form the base of the diet. They are cheap and they fill the stomach because they supply energy."),
        D("**Carbohydrates** are nutrients made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. They include sugars, starch and cellulose. "
          "Their main job is to supply **energy**.", title="Definition: carbohydrate"),
        H("Classification"),
        T("Carbohydrates are grouped by the size of their molecules. A sugar molecule is small. Starch is a very large molecule made of many glucose units joined together."),
        KEY("Three groups of carbohydrate", "", cards=[
            ["Monosaccharides (single sugars)", "Glucose, fructose (fruit sugar), galactose. Simplest sugars; sweet; dissolve in water."],
            ["Disaccharides (double sugars)", "Sucrose (table sugar, from sugar cane), maltose (malt sugar), lactose (milk sugar). Two single sugars joined."],
            ["Polysaccharides", "Starch (plant energy store), glycogen (animal energy store, in liver and muscle), cellulose (plant cell walls; fibre)."],
        ], note="Cellulose cannot be digested by humans. It is part of the dietary fibre you will study later."),
        T("Sugars taste sweet and dissolve in water. Starch does not taste sweet and does not dissolve in cold water. When we chew a piece of boiled cassava for a long time it begins to taste slightly sweet "
          "because enzymes in saliva start to change starch into sugar."),
        H("Food sources"),
        KEY("Where we find carbohydrate", "", cards=[
            ["Starchy staples", "Cassava, cocoyam, yam, plantain, Irish and sweet potato, rice, maize, millet, sorghum, bread, pasta."],
            ["Sugary foods", "Sugar, honey, sugar cane, ripe fruits (banana, mango, pineapple), sweets, soft drinks, milk (lactose)."],
        ]),
        H("Functions"),
        T("**1. Energy.** Carbohydrate is the cheapest and fastest source of energy. It gives about **17 kJ per gram**. Glucose is the fuel carried in the blood to every cell."),
        T("**2. Storage.** Extra glucose is stored as glycogen in the liver and muscles. When the stores are full, the surplus is turned into body fat."),
        T("**3. Protein sparing.** When there is enough carbohydrate, the body does not need to burn protein for energy, so protein is free to build and repair."),
        T("**4. Fibre.** Cellulose (in vegetables, fruits and whole grains) adds bulk to the food and helps the bowel to work."),
        EX("Worked example 1: energy from carbohydrate", intro="A portion of food contains 60 g of carbohydrate. Using 17 kJ per gram, calculate the energy it gives from carbohydrate.", steps=[
            S("Write the rule", text="Energy = mass of carbohydrate x energy value per gram."),
            S("Substitute", math=r"60 \times 17"),
            S("Calculate", math=r"= 1\,020 \text{ kJ}"),
        ], check=r"1\,020 \div 17 = 60"),
        H("Too much and too little"),
        T("**Too much carbohydrate**, especially sugar, taken with too little exercise, is stored as fat and can lead to **obesity** (being very overweight). Sugar also causes **tooth decay**, "
          "because bacteria in the mouth turn sugar into acid that attacks teeth. Obesity increases the risk of diabetes and heart disease."),
        T("**Too little carbohydrate** (and too little food overall) leads to weight loss, weakness, tiredness and poor concentration. If the shortage is severe and lasts, "
          "the body burns its own fat and muscle and the person may develop **marasmus**."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking sugar is the only carbohydrate", "Starch and cellulose are carbohydrates too. Cassava, rice and plantain do not taste sweet but they are rich in carbohydrate."),
        MISTAKE("Saying carbohydrates are body-building foods", "Body-building is the work of protein. Carbohydrates are energy-giving foods."),
        H("Testing foods"),
        T("**Iodine test for starch.** Add a few drops of iodine solution to the food. If starch is present the colour changes from brown to **blue-black**. "
          "Try it on a slice of raw cassava or a boiled potato."),
        T("**Benedict's test for simple sugars (reducing sugars).** Add Benedict's solution to the food solution and heat it in a water bath. "
          "A blue mixture turns green, then yellow, orange and finally **brick-red** as the amount of sugar increases. Table sugar (sucrose) must first be boiled with a little acid to give this result; check your textbook."),
        EX("Worked example 2: choosing foods", intro="Mary needs an energy-rich snack that is cheap and does not rot her teeth too much. Suggest one and explain.", steps=[
            S("Pick a starchy food", text="Roasted plantain or boiled cassava."),
            S("Explain", text="It is a cheap carbohydrate that releases energy slowly, and it has little free sugar to attack the teeth."),
            S("Add", text="Eating it with groundnuts adds protein, so the snack is more balanced."),
        ]),
        QUIZ("Which of these is a polysaccharide?", ["Glucose", "Lactose", "Starch", "Sucrose"], 2,
             "Starch is made of many glucose units, so it is a polysaccharide.", hint="Which one is a very large molecule?",
             feedback=["Glucose is a single sugar (monosaccharide).", "Lactose is a double sugar (disaccharide).", "", "Sucrose is a double sugar (disaccharide)."]),
        QUIZ("What colour does iodine solution turn when starch is present?", ["Brick-red", "Blue-black", "Purple-pink", "It stays brown"], 1,
             "Iodine turns blue-black with starch.", hint="Not Benedict's test: this one uses iodine.",
             feedback=["Brick-red is the Benedict's result for sugar.", "", "Purple-pink is not a food-test result at this level.", "If it stays brown, starch is absent."]),
        TIP("Exam link: carbohydrate classification, sources and functions appear in Paper 1 and in structured questions. A small energy calculation may be asked, so know the kJ per gram values: "
            "carbohydrate and protein about 17 kJ/g, fat about 37 kJ/g (check the values your teacher uses)."),
        PQ("(a) Name the three groups of carbohydrate. (b) Give one example of each.",
           "(a) Monosaccharides (single sugars), disaccharides (double sugars) and polysaccharides. (b) Glucose; sucrose (or lactose, maltose); starch (or cellulose, glycogen)."),
        PQ("State three functions of carbohydrate in the body.",
           "It supplies energy (about 17 kJ per gram); extra is stored as glycogen and fat; it spares protein for growth and repair; cellulose gives fibre."),
        PQ("Give three Cameroonian foods that are rich in starch.", "Cassava, plantain, cocoyam, yam, rice, maize (any three)."),
        PQ("Explain two harmful effects of eating too much sugar.",
           "It can cause obesity because the extra is stored as fat; it causes tooth decay because mouth bacteria turn sugar into acid that damages tooth enamel."),
        SUM("Carbohydrates are sugars, starch and cellulose, made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.",
            "Staples such as cassava, plantain, rice and maize are the main sources; sugar, honey and fruit give simple sugars.",
            "Functions: energy (about 17 kJ/g), storage as glycogen and fat, protein sparing, and fibre.",
            "Too much leads to obesity and tooth decay; too little leads to weakness and weight loss. Iodine turns blue-black with starch."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 3. Fats and oils
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Fats and oils",
    title="Fats and oils: concentrated energy",
    slug="fdn1-fats-and-oils",
    minutes=20,
    summary="Fats and oils are made of glycerol and fatty acids. They give the most energy per gram (about 37 kJ), insulate the body and carry vitamins A, D, E and K.",
    objectives=[
        "Define fats and oils and distinguish saturated from unsaturated fats",
        "Name sources of fat, including palm oil and groundnut oil",
        "State the functions of fat and the effects of too much or too little",
        "Calculate the energy in a food from its protein, carbohydrate and fat",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Fat", "Oil", "Glycerol", "Fatty acid", "Saturated fat", "Unsaturated fat", "Cholesterol", "kJ per gram"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Palm oil gives colour and taste to ndole, ekwang and many soups. Groundnut oil fries our beignets. The fat on a piece of meat melts when it is cooked. "
          "All of these are **lipids**, the group of nutrients we call fats and oils."),
        D("**Fats** and **oils** are nutrients made of carbon, hydrogen and oxygen. A **fat** is solid at room temperature (butter, lard). "
          "An **oil** is liquid at room temperature (groundnut oil, palm oil in a warm place is soft). Chemically they are made of one **glycerol** molecule joined to three **fatty acids**.",
          title="Definition: fats and oils"),
        H("Saturated and unsaturated fats"),
        T("Fatty acids can be 'saturated' or 'unsaturated'. You do not need the chemistry. You need to know where each kind is found and what each does to health."),
        KEY("Two kinds of fat", "", cards=[
            ["Saturated fats", "Usually solid. Found in meat fat, butter, cheese, lard, coconut oil and palm oil. Too much raises blood cholesterol and the risk of heart disease."],
            ["Unsaturated fats", "Usually liquid oils. Found in groundnut oil, soya oil, sunflower oil, avocado and oily fish. Better for the heart when used in moderation."],
        ], note="Textbooks differ on how to describe palm oil: it is semi-solid and fairly high in saturated fat. Check your textbook."),
        T("**Cholesterol** is a fatty substance found in animal foods such as egg yolk, liver and meat fat. The body also makes it. Too much cholesterol in the blood can block the arteries."),
        H("Sources"),
        KEY("Where we find fat", "", cards=[
            ["Visible fats", "Cooking oils, margarine, butter, the fat on meat, skin of chicken."],
            ["Hidden fats", "Groundnuts, egusi seeds, avocado, fish (especially oily fish like mackerel), eggs, milk, cheese, fried foods, pastries."],
            ["Plant oils", "Palm oil, groundnut oil, soya oil, cotton-seed oil, coconut oil."],
        ], note="Red palm oil also carries vitamin A (carotene), so it is more than just a fuel."),
        H("Functions"),
        KEY("What fat does in the body", "", cards=[
            ["Energy", "Fat gives about 37 kJ per gram, more than twice the energy of carbohydrate or protein. It is a concentrated energy source."],
            ["Insulation", "A layer of fat under the skin keeps the body warm."],
            ["Protection", "Fat around the kidneys and other organs cushions them from knocks."],
            ["Vitamins", "Fat carries and helps absorb the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E and K."],
            ["Flavour and fullness", "Fat gives taste and makes meals filling because it is digested slowly."],
        ]),
        T("The body also needs small amounts of certain **essential fatty acids** that it cannot make. They come from plant oils and fish."),
        H("Calculating energy"),
        T("Energy is measured in **kilojoules (kJ)**. We use these values: protein 17 kJ per gram, carbohydrate 17 kJ per gram, fat 37 kJ per gram. "
          "(Some books use 16 or 17 for protein and carbohydrate and 37 or 38 for fat. Use the values your teacher gives you.)"),
        EX("Worked example 1: energy in a snack", intro="A snack contains 10 g of protein, 30 g of carbohydrate and 12 g of fat (invented for practice). Calculate its total energy value.", steps=[
            S("Energy from protein", math=r"10 \times 17 = 170 \text{ kJ}"),
            S("Energy from carbohydrate", math=r"30 \times 17 = 510 \text{ kJ}"),
            S("Energy from fat", math=r"12 \times 37 = 444 \text{ kJ}"),
            S("Add", math=r"170 + 510 + 444 = 1\,124 \text{ kJ}"),
        ], check=r"1\,124 \text{ kJ in total}"),
        EX("Worked example 2: how much more energy does fat give?", intro="Compare 10 g of fat with 10 g of carbohydrate.", steps=[
            S("Fat", math=r"10 \times 37 = 370 \text{ kJ}"),
            S("Carbohydrate", math=r"10 \times 17 = 170 \text{ kJ}"),
            S("Difference", math=r"370 - 170 = 200 \text{ kJ}", text="Fat gives 200 kJ more, a little more than twice as much."),
        ]),
        H("Too much and too little"),
        T("**Too much fat** is stored under the skin and causes **obesity**. A diet high in saturated fat can raise cholesterol and lead to heart disease and high blood pressure."),
        T("**Too little fat** causes weight loss and lack of energy, and a shortage of the fat-soluble vitamins (for example, night blindness from lack of vitamin A). "
          "Very little fat in the diet of young children is a problem because they need a lot of energy in small meals."),
        MISTAKE("Saying fat gives the same energy as carbohydrate", "Per gram, fat gives about 37 kJ and carbohydrate about 17 kJ. That is why a small amount of oil adds a lot of energy to a meal."),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up fat and oil", "Both are fats in the chemical sense. In everyday words, a fat is solid at room temperature and an oil is liquid."),
        QUIZ("Which nutrient gives the most energy per gram?", ["Fat", "Protein", "Carbohydrate", "Water"], 0,
             "Fat gives about 37 kJ per gram, protein and carbohydrate about 17 kJ per gram, and water gives none.", hint="Think of 'concentrated energy'.",
             feedback=["", "Protein gives about 17 kJ per gram.", "Carbohydrate gives about 17 kJ per gram.", "Water has no energy value."]),
        QUIZ("Which vitamins are carried by fat in the diet?", ["B and C", "A, D, E and K", "B12 and C", "Only vitamin C"], 1,
             "Vitamins A, D, E and K are fat-soluble.", hint="The fat-soluble group has four vitamins.",
             feedback=["B and C are water-soluble.", "", "B12 and C are water-soluble.", "Vitamin C dissolves in water, not fat."]),
        TIP("Exam link: functions and sources of fat are common in Paper 1. Energy calculations can appear in Paper 2. Always show the multiplication for each nutrient and add the parts."),
        PQ("(a) State two functions of fat. (b) Name two food sources of saturated fat.",
           "(a) Energy (37 kJ per gram); insulation under the skin; protects organs; carries vitamins A, D, E and K. (b) Butter, meat fat, cheese, lard, coconut oil, palm oil."),
        PQ("Calculate the energy in 8 g of protein, 20 g of carbohydrate and 5 g of fat (invented for practice).",
           "Protein 8 x 17 = 136 kJ. Carbohydrate 20 x 17 = 340 kJ. Fat 5 x 37 = 185 kJ. Total 136 + 340 + 185 = 661 kJ."),
        PQ("Why should a person who is overweight reduce fried foods?", "Fat is very rich in energy, and fried foods add much fat. Extra energy is stored as body fat, so cutting fried food reduces energy intake and helps weight loss."),
        PQ("Give two effects of eating too much saturated fat.", "It raises blood cholesterol and can block the arteries (heart disease); it can cause obesity."),
        SUM("Fats and oils are made of glycerol and fatty acids; fats are solid and oils are liquid at room temperature.",
            "Sources: oils, margarine, butter, meat fat, groundnuts, egusi, avocado, oily fish and eggs.",
            "Functions: concentrated energy (about 37 kJ/g), insulation, protection of organs, carrying vitamins A, D, E and K.",
            "Too much leads to obesity and heart disease; too little causes weight loss and fat-soluble vitamin deficiency."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 4. Vitamins
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Vitamins",
    title="Vitamins: A, B group, C, D, E and K",
    slug="fdn1-vitamins",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Vitamins are protective nutrients needed in tiny amounts. Know the sources, functions and deficiency disease of vitamins A, B1, B2, B3, B12, C, D, E and K.",
    objectives=[
        "Distinguish fat-soluble from water-soluble vitamins",
        "State the main sources and functions of each vitamin on the syllabus",
        "Name the deficiency disease for each vitamin",
        "Explain how cooking and storage can destroy vitamins",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Vitamin", "Fat-soluble", "Water-soluble", "Night blindness", "Beriberi", "Pellagra", "Scurvy", "Rickets"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A fisherman in Limbe eats fish and fresh fruit and rarely gets sick. A child fed only on plain starch may get sore gums or bad eyesight. "
          "The difference is often **vitamins**: substances needed in very small amounts to keep the body working and protected."),
        D("**Vitamins** are organic nutrients needed in very small amounts for growth, health and protection against disease. "
          "Most cannot be made by the body, so they must come from food.", title="Definition: vitamin"),
        KEY("Two groups of vitamins", "", cards=[
            ["Fat-soluble: A, D, E, K", "Dissolve in fat. Found in fatty foods. Can be stored in the liver. Not easily lost in cooking water."],
            ["Water-soluble: B group and C", "Dissolve in water. Not stored well, so needed every day. Easily lost in cooking water and by heat."],
        ]),
        H("The fat-soluble vitamins"),
        EX("Vitamin A (retinol)", intro="Sources, functions and deficiency.", steps=[
            S("Sources", text="Liver, eggs, milk, red palm oil, orange and yellow foods (carrot, ripe mango, pawpaw, sweet potato) and dark green leaves such as eru and spinach."),
            S("Functions", text="Sight in dim light; healthy skin and the linings of the nose, mouth and gut; growth; resistance to infection."),
            S("Deficiency", text="Night blindness (poor sight in dim light). Severe lack dries the eye surface (xerophthalmia) and can cause blindness."),
        ]),
        EX("Vitamin D (calciferol)", intro="Sources, functions and deficiency.", steps=[
            S("Sources", text="Oily fish, eggs, liver, margarine with added vitamin D. The skin also makes it when sunlight falls on it."),
            S("Function", text="Helps the body absorb calcium and phosphorus to build strong bones and teeth."),
            S("Deficiency", text="Rickets in children (soft, bent leg bones) and osteomalacia (soft bones) in adults."),
        ]),
        T("**Vitamin E** acts as an antioxidant: it protects body cells from damage. It is found in vegetable oils, nuts, seeds and whole grains. A true deficiency is rare."),
        T("**Vitamin K** helps the blood to **clot** after a cut. It is found in green leafy vegetables and liver, and gut bacteria also make some. Lack of it causes prolonged bleeding. Newborn babies are often given vitamin K to prevent bleeding."),
        H("The water-soluble vitamins"),
        EX("B group vitamins", intro="Thiamine (B1), riboflavin (B2), niacin (B3) and B12.", steps=[
            S("B1 (thiamine)", text="Helps release energy from carbohydrate and keeps nerves healthy. Sources: whole grains, beans, groundnuts, pork. Deficiency: beriberi (weak muscles, nerve damage, tiredness). Lost when rice or flour is highly polished."),
            S("B2 (riboflavin)", text="Helps release energy; keeps skin and mouth healthy. Sources: milk, eggs, liver, green vegetables. Deficiency: cracked corners of the mouth and a sore tongue. Destroyed by light."),
            S("B3 (niacin)", text="Helps release energy; keeps skin, gut and nerves healthy. Sources: meat, fish, groundnuts, whole grains. Deficiency: pellagra (skin rash, diarrhoea, confusion), seen where the diet is mostly maize."),
            S("B12", text="Needed to make red blood cells and for nerves. Found only in animal foods: meat, fish, eggs, milk. Deficiency: a type of anaemia (pernicious anaemia). People who eat no animal foods need a supplement."),
        ]),
        EX("Vitamin C (ascorbic acid)", intro="Sources, functions and deficiency.", steps=[
            S("Sources", text="Oranges, lemons, guava, pawpaw, pineapple, tomato, green pepper and fresh green leaves."),
            S("Functions", text="Keeps gums, skin and blood vessels healthy and heals wounds; helps the body absorb iron; helps fight infection."),
            S("Deficiency", text="Scurvy: bleeding and swollen gums, slow healing of wounds, tiredness and sore joints."),
        ]),
        H("Protecting vitamins in the kitchen"),
        T("Water-soluble vitamins, especially vitamin C, are easily lost. They dissolve into the cooking water and are destroyed by heat, air and long storage. To save them:"),
        KEY("Four ways to save vitamins", "", cards=[
            ["Buy and eat fresh", "Use fruit and vegetables soon after buying them."],
            ["Cut late", "Wash vegetables whole, then cut them just before cooking."],
            ["Cook briefly", "Use a little water, a short time and a lid; use the cooking water in soup or sauce."],
            ["Avoid soda", "Do not add bicarbonate of soda to green vegetables. Eat fruit raw when possible."],
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up vitamin deficiency diseases", "Remember by organ or sign: A = eyes (night blindness), C = gums (scurvy), D = bones (rickets), B1 = nerves (beriberi), B3 = skin (pellagra)."),
        MISTAKE("Saying vitamin D comes only from food", "The skin makes vitamin D when sunlight falls on it. Food sources add to this."),
        QUIZ("A child has soft, bent leg bones. Which vitamin is lacking?", ["Vitamin A", "Vitamin C", "Vitamin D", "Vitamin K"], 2,
             "Vitamin D is needed for the body to absorb calcium for strong bones. Lack of it causes rickets.", hint="Think of bones and sunlight.",
             feedback=["Lack of vitamin A causes night blindness.", "Lack of vitamin C causes scurvy, with bleeding gums.", "", "Lack of vitamin K affects blood clotting."]),
        QUIZ("Which of these vitamins is found only in animal foods?", ["Vitamin C", "Vitamin B12", "Vitamin E", "Vitamin K"], 1,
             "B12 is found in meat, fish, eggs and milk, and is not found in plant foods.", hint="Think of vegetarians.",
             feedback=["Vitamin C is found in fruits and vegetables.", "", "Vitamin E is found in vegetable oils and nuts.", "Vitamin K is found in green leafy vegetables."]),
        TIP("Exam link: the vitamin table (source, function, deficiency) is one of the most popular topics in Paper 1 and Paper 2. Learn one disease per vitamin and one Cameroonian source each."),
        PQ("Name the deficiency disease caused by a lack of (a) vitamin C, (b) vitamin D, (c) vitamin B1, (d) vitamin A.",
           "(a) Scurvy. (b) Rickets (osteomalacia in adults). (c) Beriberi. (d) Night blindness."),
        PQ("State two functions of vitamin C and two good sources in Cameroon.",
           "Functions: heals wounds and keeps gums and skin healthy; helps absorb iron; helps resist infection. Sources: oranges, guava, pawpaw, tomatoes, green pepper."),
        PQ("Explain why vitamin C is often lost when vegetables are cooked.",
           "Vitamin C dissolves in water and is destroyed by heat and air. Long cooking in plenty of water, or cutting and keeping vegetables before cooking, leaves little in the food."),
        PQ("Name two ways to keep vitamins when cooking vegetables.", "Cook in a small amount of water for a short time with the lid on; do not cut vegetables too early; use the cooking water; do not add bicarbonate of soda."),
        SUM("Vitamins are needed in tiny amounts. A, D, E and K are fat-soluble; the B group and C are water-soluble.",
            "A (night blindness), B1 (beriberi), B3 (pellagra), C (scurvy), D (rickets) each have a deficiency disease; B12 lack gives anaemia and K lack gives poor clotting.",
            "Animal foods, palm oil, fruits, green leaves and whole grains are good vitamin sources.",
            "Water-soluble vitamins are lost by long cooking, much water and long storage."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 5. Minerals
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Minerals",
    title="Minerals: calcium, phosphorus, iron, iodine, sodium and fluoride",
    slug="fdn1-minerals",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Minerals are inorganic nutrients. Calcium and phosphorus build bones, iron makes blood, iodine prevents goitre, sodium balances body fluids and fluoride protects teeth.",
    objectives=[
        "Define minerals and state why the body needs them",
        "State the sources, functions and deficiency effects of calcium, phosphorus, iron, iodine, sodium and fluoride",
        "Explain how to improve iron absorption",
        "Describe the dangers of too much salt",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Mineral", "Calcium", "Phosphorus", "Iron", "Anaemia", "Iodine", "Goitre", "Sodium", "Fluoride"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A girl in Maroua feels weak and tired and her eyelids look pale. A farmer in a mountain village has a swollen neck. Both may be suffering from a lack of a **mineral**: iron in the first case, iodine in the second."),
        D("**Minerals** are inorganic nutrients (not made by living things) that the body needs in small amounts to build bones and teeth, make body fluids and blood, and help other body processes work.",
          title="Definition: minerals"),
        H("Calcium (Ca) and phosphorus (P)"),
        KEY("Calcium", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Milk, cheese, small fish eaten with their bones, green leaves, beans, groundnuts."],
            ["Functions", "Builds strong bones and teeth; helps blood clot; helps muscles and nerves work."],
            ["Deficiency", "Weak bones and teeth, rickets in children, and brittle bones (osteoporosis) in older people."],
        ], note="Vitamin D is needed so that the body can absorb calcium."),
        KEY("Phosphorus", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Found in almost all foods: milk, meat, fish, eggs, beans, cereals."],
            ["Functions", "Works with calcium in bones and teeth; helps release energy in cells."],
            ["Deficiency", "Rare, because it is so widely found. Weak bones if calcium and vitamin D are short too."],
        ]),
        H("Iron (Fe)"),
        T("Iron is needed to make **haemoglobin**, the red substance in red blood cells that carries oxygen around the body."),
        KEY("Iron", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Liver, red meat, fish, eggs, beans, dark green leaves (such as eru and bitterleaf), whole grains, dried fruit."],
            ["Functions", "Makes haemoglobin; carries oxygen in the blood."],
            ["Deficiency", "Anaemia: pale skin, tiredness, breathlessness, dizziness. Common in girls, pregnant women and young children."],
        ]),
        TIP("Vitamin C helps the body absorb iron from plant foods. Eat beans or leaves with a fruit or tomato. Strong tea or coffee taken with a meal makes it harder to absorb iron."),
        H("Iodine (I)"),
        KEY("Iodine", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Sea fish and seafood, iodised salt, and plants grown in soil that contains iodine."],
            ["Functions", "Needed to make thyroxine, a hormone from the thyroid gland in the neck, which controls growth and the rate at which the body uses energy."],
            ["Deficiency", "Goitre (swelling of the thyroid in the neck). In a pregnant woman, severe lack can harm the baby's brain."],
        ], note="Many countries add iodine to table salt. Check the labels of the salt you buy."),
        H("Sodium (Na) and fluoride (F)"),
        KEY("Sodium", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Table salt, stock cubes, salted and smoked fish, bread, cheese, processed foods."],
            ["Functions", "Keeps the right amount of water in the body; helps nerves and muscles work."],
            ["Too much or too little", "Too little (heavy sweating or diarrhoea) causes muscle cramps. Too much salt over years can raise blood pressure."],
        ]),
        KEY("Fluoride", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Fluoridated water or toothpaste, tea, fish eaten with bones, some drinking waters."],
            ["Functions", "Makes tooth enamel stronger so that teeth resist decay."],
            ["Too much", "A lot over many years can leave white or brown marks on teeth (mottling). Check the fluoride in your area's water with a health worker."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: matching mineral to problem", intro="State the mineral lacking in each case: (a) a swollen neck, (b) pale and tired, (c) soft bones, (d) muscle cramps after heavy sweating.", steps=[
            S("(a) Swollen neck", text="Iodine: goitre."),
            S("(b) Pale and tired", text="Iron: anaemia."),
            S("(c) Soft bones", text="Calcium (with vitamin D): rickets."),
            S("(d) Cramps", text="Sodium (salt) lost in sweat."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: advice for a pregnant woman", intro="Give diet advice that supplies iron and calcium.", steps=[
            S("Iron", text="Eat liver or fish, beans and dark green leaves with a fruit rich in vitamin C."),
            S("Calcium", text="Drink milk, eat small fish with their bones and groundnuts."),
            S("Explain", text="Iron makes extra blood for mother and baby; calcium builds the baby's bones and keeps the mother's bones strong."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Mixing up iron and iodine", "Iron makes haemoglobin and lack causes anaemia. Iodine makes thyroxine and lack causes goitre. Their names look alike, so link each to its disease."),
        MISTAKE("Thinking all salt is bad", "The body needs sodium. The problem is eating too much of it every day, especially from stock cubes and salted foods."),
        QUIZ("Which mineral helps the blood to carry oxygen?", ["Calcium", "Iodine", "Iron", "Fluoride"], 2,
             "Iron is part of haemoglobin in red blood cells.", hint="Think of anaemia.",
             feedback=["Calcium is for bones, teeth and clotting.", "Iodine is for the thyroid hormone.", "", "Fluoride protects teeth."]),
        QUIZ("What does a lack of iodine cause?", ["Rickets", "Goitre", "Scurvy", "Anaemia"], 1,
             "Without iodine the thyroid cannot make thyroxine, and the gland swells to form a goitre.", hint="Look at the neck.",
             feedback=["Rickets comes from lack of vitamin D and calcium.", "", "Scurvy comes from lack of vitamin C.", "Anaemia comes from lack of iron."]),
        TIP("Exam link: mineral sources and deficiencies appear in Paper 1 and in Paper 2 structured questions, often together with vitamins. A table with source, function and deficiency is the best way to revise."),
        PQ("(a) State two functions of calcium. (b) Name two Cameroonian foods rich in calcium.", "(a) Builds strong bones and teeth; helps blood clot; helps muscles and nerves. (b) Milk, small fish eaten with their bones, green leaves, beans."),
        PQ("Explain how a diet lacking iron causes tiredness.", "Iron is needed to make haemoglobin which carries oxygen. Without enough, the blood carries less oxygen to the muscles and brain, so the person gets anaemia and feels weak and tired."),
        PQ("State the function of iodine and the disease caused by its lack.", "Iodine is used to make thyroxine in the thyroid gland, which controls growth and energy use. Lack causes goitre."),
        PQ("Give two reasons why a person should not eat too much salt.", "Too much sodium can raise blood pressure and strain the heart and kidneys; salty foods can also lead to fluid retention."),
        SUM("Minerals are inorganic nutrients needed in small amounts.",
            "Calcium and phosphorus build bones and teeth; iron makes haemoglobin (lack causes anaemia); iodine makes thyroxine (lack causes goitre).",
            "Sodium controls body water and lack gives cramps; too much raises blood pressure; fluoride protects tooth enamel.",
            "Vitamin C improves iron absorption; vitamin D improves calcium absorption."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 6. Water and dietary fibre
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Water and dietary fibre",
    title="Water and dietary fibre (NSP)",
    slug="fdn1-water-and-fibre",
    minutes=18,
    summary="Water makes up most of the body and is needed for every process. Dietary fibre (NSP) is plant material we cannot digest; it adds bulk and prevents constipation.",
    objectives=[
        "State the functions and sources of water and how the body loses it",
        "Describe dehydration and the use of oral rehydration",
        "Define dietary fibre (non-starch polysaccharide, NSP) and name sources",
        "State the functions of fibre and the effect of too little",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Water", "Dehydration", "Oral rehydration", "Dietary fibre", "NSP", "Cellulose", "Constipation"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A farmer working in the sun in Garoua may sweat for hours. By the afternoon his mouth is dry and he feels dizzy. A person can live for weeks without food but only a few days without water. "
          "Water is a nutrient even though it gives no energy."),
        D("About **two-thirds** of the human body is water (roughly 60 per cent in an adult). Blood, saliva and every cell contain water.", title="Fact: water in the body"),
        H("Functions of water"),
        KEY("What water does", "", cards=[
            ["Solvent and transport", "Dissolves nutrients and carries them in blood. Carries waste out in urine."],
            ["Digestion and reactions", "Digestive juices are mostly water, and the chemical reactions of the cells happen in water."],
            ["Temperature control", "Sweat evaporates from the skin and cools the body."],
            ["Lubrication", "Saliva, mucus and joint fluid are mostly water."],
        ]),
        T("**Sources of water**: drinks (clean water, tea, juice), soups and sauces, fruits and vegetables (pineapple, watermelon, tomatoes and cucumber contain a lot) and even cooked rice or porridge. "
          "Some water is also made inside the body when food is used in cells."),
        T("**Water loss**: the body loses water in urine, in sweat, in the air we breathe out and in faeces. In hot weather, during exercise or with diarrhoea and vomiting, we lose much more. "
          "Adults usually need about **1.5 to 2 litres** of fluid a day, more in hot weather (check the advice of a health worker)."),
        D("**Dehydration** is a harmful loss of water from the body. Signs: thirst, dry mouth, dark and little urine, headache, dizziness and, in babies, a sunken soft spot on the head. "
          "Severe diarrhoea can dehydrate a child quickly.", title="Definition: dehydration"),
        TIP("For a child with diarrhoea, give **oral rehydration solution (ORS)**, a safe mix of clean water, salt and sugar. ORS packets are sold at pharmacies and health centres. Keep giving breast milk or food and seek a health worker if the child gets worse."),
        H("Dietary fibre (NSP)"),
        D("**Dietary fibre**, also called **non-starch polysaccharide (NSP)**, is the part of plant foods that the human gut cannot digest. It is mostly **cellulose**, the material of plant cell walls.", title="Definition: dietary fibre"),
        T("Fibre is not absorbed into the blood, so it gives no energy. Yet it is very important for health."),
        KEY("Fibre", "", cards=[
            ["Sources", "Whole grains (whole maize, brown rice, millet), beans and peas, vegetables, fruits with their skins, nuts, plantain and cocoyam."],
            ["Functions", "Adds bulk to the faeces and keeps food moving through the gut; prevents constipation; helps prevent bowel disease; helps you feel full, which helps control weight."],
            ["Too little", "Constipation, piles (haemorrhoids) and a higher risk of bowel disease."],
        ], note="Fibre absorbs water. If you eat more fibre, drink more water too."),
        EX("Worked example 1: improving fibre in a meal", intro="A family eats white rice and fried fish daily. Suggest two changes that add fibre.", steps=[
            S("Add vegetables", text="Add leaves such as okra or a salad."),
            S("Change the starch", text="Use brown rice, or serve beans with the rice, or use whole maize."),
            S("Explain", text="These foods contain cellulose that adds bulk and prevents constipation."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: water balance", intro="Explain why a worker in a hot place needs extra water.", steps=[
            S("Loss", text="Heat makes the body sweat to keep cool, and sweat is mostly water."),
            S("Effect", text="Without replacement the body becomes dehydrated: thirst, headache, dizziness."),
            S("Advice", text="Drink clean water often during the day, and eat fruit and vegetable foods that contain water."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying fibre gives energy", "Fibre is not digested, so it is not a source of energy. Its value is that it adds bulk and helps the gut."),
        MISTAKE("Saying water is not a nutrient", "Water is an essential nutrient. It gives no energy but the body cannot work without it."),
        QUIZ("Which food is a good source of dietary fibre?", ["Refined white sugar", "Beans", "Cooking oil", "Boiled egg white"], 1,
             "Beans are rich in fibre. Sugar and oil have none, and egg white is protein.", hint="Which one is a plant seed with its coat?",
             feedback=["Refined sugar has no fibre.", "", "Oil is pure fat and has no fibre.", "Egg white is protein and has no fibre."]),
        QUIZ("Which of these is a function of dietary fibre?", ["Gives much energy", "Prevents constipation", "Builds bones", "Carries oxygen"], 1,
             "Fibre adds bulk to the faeces and prevents constipation.", hint="Think of the bowel.",
             feedback=["Fibre is not digested, so it gives no energy.", "", "Calcium and vitamin D build bones.", "Iron in haemoglobin carries oxygen."]),
        TIP("Exam link: water and fibre often appear as one-mark recall items in Paper 1 and as part of a Paper 2 question on a balanced diet."),
        PQ("State three functions of water in the body.", "Dissolves and transports nutrients and waste; takes part in digestion and chemical reactions; cools the body through sweat; lubricates joints and the gut."),
        PQ("(a) What is dietary fibre? (b) Name three sources.", "(a) The part of plant foods that cannot be digested, mostly cellulose (NSP). (b) Whole maize, brown rice, beans, vegetables, fruit with skin."),
        PQ("Describe two signs of dehydration.", "Thirst and dry mouth; dark, little urine; headache and dizziness; sunken soft spot on a baby's head."),
        PQ("Explain why a diet lacking fibre may cause constipation.", "Fibre adds bulk and holds water in the faeces so they pass easily. Without it the faeces are small and hard and move slowly."),
        SUM("Water is about two-thirds of the body and is needed as a solvent, for transport, digestion, cooling and lubrication.",
            "Lose water by urine, sweat, breath and faeces; replace it with drinks, soup and fruit; ORS helps in diarrhoea.",
            "Dietary fibre (NSP, mostly cellulose) is plant material we cannot digest.",
            "Fibre prevents constipation and bowel disease; sources are whole grains, beans, vegetables and fruit."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 7. Digestion and absorption
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M1, topic="Digestion and absorption",
    title="Digestion and absorption of food",
    slug="fdn1-digestion-absorption",
    minutes=25,
    summary="Digestion breaks food into small soluble molecules using teeth and enzymes along the alimentary canal. Absorption into the blood happens mainly in the small intestine.",
    objectives=[
        "Define digestion and absorption",
        "Name the parts of the alimentary canal and their roles",
        "State the enzymes, where they act and the end products of digestion",
        "Describe how the small intestine is adapted for absorption",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Digestion", "Enzyme", "Alimentary canal", "Amylase", "Pepsin", "Lipase", "Bile", "Villi", "Absorption", "Egestion"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("When you eat a piece of boiled yam or fish, the food is too big to enter your blood. The body must first break it into tiny pieces. This is **digestion**."),
        D("**Digestion** is the breakdown of large insoluble food molecules into small soluble molecules that the body can absorb. "
          "**Absorption** is the movement of these small molecules from the gut into the blood.", title="Definition: digestion and absorption"),
        T("Digestion has two parts. **Mechanical digestion** breaks food into smaller pieces by chewing and churning. **Chemical digestion** uses **enzymes**, special proteins that speed up the breakdown of food, to split the food molecules."),
        H("The alimentary canal"),
        IMG("f5fdn1-digestive-system.svg", alt="Schematic of the digestive system: mouth, gullet, stomach, liver, pancreas, small intestine, large intestine and anus, all labelled.",
            caption="Figure 1. The alimentary canal and its organs (schematic, not to scale)."),
        T("The **alimentary canal** (gut) is a long tube from the mouth to the anus. Food passes along it by **peristalsis**: waves of muscle squeezing behind the food. The liver and pancreas are not part of the tube but they make juices that help digestion."),
        H("What happens at each stage"),
        KEY("Mouth and stomach", "", cards=[
            ["Mouth", "Teeth chew food (mechanical). Saliva contains **amylase**, which starts to change starch into maltose. The tongue rolls food into a ball (bolus) that is swallowed."],
            ["Stomach", "Muscles churn the food. Gastric juice has hydrochloric acid (kills germs, makes acid) and **pepsin**, which begins to break protein into shorter chains. Food stays for hours."],
        ]),
        KEY("Small intestine", "", cards=[
            ["Bile", "Made in the liver, stored in the gall bladder. It does not contain enzymes. It **emulsifies** fat (breaks it into tiny droplets) so that lipase can work."],
            ["Pancreatic juice", "From the pancreas: amylase (starch), trypsin (protein) and lipase (fat)."],
            ["Intestinal juice", "From the wall of the small intestine: enzymes that finish digesting maltose to glucose and chains of amino acids to amino acids."],
        ]),
        CODE("""Food       Enzyme             Product
Starch     amylase            maltose, then glucose
Protein    pepsin, trypsin    amino acids
Fat        lipase             fatty acids + glycerol""", "text", title="Table 1. Enzymes and end products"),
        T("**End products of digestion:** carbohydrates give **glucose**; proteins give **amino acids**; fats give **fatty acids and glycerol**."),
        H("Absorption and the large intestine"),
        T("Absorption takes place mainly in the **small intestine** (the ileum). Its inside wall is covered with millions of tiny finger-like **villi**. They give a very large surface area, have thin walls and a rich blood supply, so absorption is fast."),
        T("Glucose, amino acids, vitamins and minerals pass into the **blood** capillaries in each villus. Fatty acids and glycerol pass into a **lacteal** (a lymph vessel) and reach the blood later. The blood takes the nutrients to the liver and then to all the cells."),
        T("Material that cannot be digested, such as fibre, passes into the **large intestine**. There, much of the **water** is absorbed. The remaining waste becomes faeces, stored in the rectum and passed out through the anus. Passing out undigested waste is called **egestion**."),
        EX("Worked example 1: following a meal of rice and fish", intro="Describe what happens to the starch in the rice and the protein in the fish.", steps=[
            S("Mouth", text="Saliva amylase starts to change starch to maltose."),
            S("Stomach", text="Pepsin and acid start protein digestion; churning mixes the food."),
            S("Small intestine", text="Pancreatic amylase and intestinal enzymes finish starch to glucose. Trypsin and peptidases finish protein to amino acids."),
            S("Absorption", text="Glucose and amino acids pass through the villi into the blood."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: why do villi help?", intro="Explain how the small intestine is adapted for absorption.", steps=[
            S("Surface area", text="Villi greatly increase the area for absorbing."),
            S("Thin wall", text="Each villus is only one cell thick, so nutrients move across quickly."),
            S("Blood supply", text="A network of capillaries takes absorbed nutrients away at once, so more can be absorbed."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying bile is an enzyme", "Bile contains no enzyme. It breaks fat into droplets (emulsifies) to make digestion by lipase faster."),
        MISTAKE("Saying most absorption happens in the stomach", "Most absorption happens in the small intestine. The large intestine absorbs mainly water."),
        QUIZ("Which enzyme in saliva starts the digestion of starch?", ["Pepsin", "Lipase", "Amylase", "Trypsin"], 2,
             "Amylase in saliva changes starch into maltose.", hint="Think of chewing cassava until it tastes sweet.",
             feedback=["Pepsin acts on protein in the stomach.", "Lipase acts on fat.", "", "Trypsin acts on protein in the small intestine."]),
        QUIZ("What are the end products of protein digestion?", ["Glucose", "Amino acids", "Fatty acids", "Maltose"], 1,
             "Proteins are broken into amino acids.", hint="Proteins are made of these units.",
             feedback=["Glucose comes from carbohydrate.", "", "Fatty acids come from fats.", "Maltose is an intermediate product of starch."]),
        TIP("Exam link: digestion and absorption appear in Paper 1 and as a Paper 2 question; the Board's report says that digestion is popular and well answered, so aim for precise enzyme names and end products. You may be asked to label the alimentary canal."),
        PQ("(a) Define digestion. (b) Name two organs that make digestive juices but are not part of the alimentary canal.", "(a) The breakdown of large insoluble food into small soluble molecules that can be absorbed. (b) Liver (bile) and pancreas (pancreatic juice)."),
        PQ("State the end products of the digestion of (a) starch, (b) fat.", "(a) Glucose. (b) Fatty acids and glycerol."),
        PQ("Explain the role of bile in digestion.", "Bile is made by the liver and stored in the gall bladder. It emulsifies fat into small droplets, giving a larger surface for lipase to act on."),
        PQ("Describe two features of the small intestine that make it good at absorbing food.", "It is long; its wall has millions of villi that increase the surface area; the villi have thin walls and a rich blood supply."),
        SUM("Digestion breaks big insoluble food molecules into small soluble ones; absorption moves them into the blood.",
            "Starch goes to glucose (amylase), protein to amino acids (pepsin, trypsin) and fat to fatty acids and glycerol (lipase, with bile).",
            "Most absorption is in the small intestine through villi; the large intestine absorbs water.",
            "Undigested waste leaves the body as faeces by egestion."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 8. Kitchen layout and design
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Kitchen layout and design",
    title="Kitchen layouts, ventilation, lighting and flooring",
    slug="fdn1-kitchen-layout",
    minutes=22,
    summary="A good kitchen is planned around the work triangle of sink, cooker and refrigerator. Know the four layouts and the needs for ventilation, lighting and flooring.",
    objectives=[
        "Describe the single-wall, L-shaped, U-shaped and parallel kitchen layouts",
        "Explain the work triangle and why it saves effort",
        "State the requirements for ventilation, lighting and flooring",
        "Choose a layout for a given space and family",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Work triangle", "Single-wall kitchen", "L-shaped", "U-shaped", "Parallel (galley)", "Ventilation", "Non-slip flooring", "Work surface"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("In many Cameroonian homes the kitchen is a small room or an outside shelter. A cook who must walk far between the water, the fire and the food store wastes time and energy and may have accidents. "
          "A well-planned kitchen is safe, hygienic and saves effort."),
        T("Planning a kitchen means asking: what work is done here, how many people work at one time, how much space is there, and what money is available."),
        H("The work triangle"),
        D("The **work triangle** is the triangle formed by the three main work centres: the **refrigerator** (storage), the **sink** (preparation and washing) and the **cooker** (cooking). "
          "The cook moves between them all day, so they should be close together but with enough work surface between them.", title="Definition: work triangle"),
        T("A good rule is that the three sides of the triangle add up to about 4 to 6 metres in total (check the figure your textbook gives), and that no cupboard or traffic path crosses it. "
          "Too short and there is no room to work; too long and the cook walks too far."),
        H("The four layouts"),
        IMG("f5fdn1-kitchen-layouts.svg", alt="Plan views of four kitchens: A single-wall, B L-shaped, C U-shaped and D parallel, each showing the positions of refrigerator F, sink S and cooker C.",
            caption="Figure 1. Four kitchen layouts (schematic plans). F = refrigerator, S = sink, C = cooker."),
        KEY("Layouts compared", "", cards=[
            ["Single-wall", "All units along one wall. Suits a small room or one cook. No real triangle, so the cook walks along the line. Cheap to plan."],
            ["L-shaped", "Units along two adjacent walls. Good triangle, flexible, leaves space for a table. The corner can be wasted unless used for storage."],
            ["U-shaped", "Units along three walls. Gives the best work triangle and most storage and worktop. Needs a bigger room and has no through traffic."],
            ["Parallel (galley)", "Units along two facing walls with a passage between. Efficient but traffic passing through can disturb the cook, and the passage must be wide enough for safe movement."],
        ]),
        H("Ventilation, lighting and flooring"),
        KEY("Ventilation", "Cooking makes heat, steam, smoke, smells and sometimes gas fumes. These must be removed.", cards=[
            ["How", "Large windows and doors opposite each other for a cross-draught; air bricks or vents; an extractor fan or a cooker hood; a chimney for wood or charcoal."],
            ["Why", "Prevents a stuffy kitchen, reduces condensation and mould, removes smoke that harms the lungs, and stops dangerous gas building up."],
        ]),
        KEY("Lighting", "", cards=[
            ["Natural", "Windows placed over the sink and worktops give free daylight. Avoid strong glare."],
            ["Artificial", "Bright, shadow-free lights over the sink, cooker and worktops so that the cook can see knives and food safely."],
        ]),
        KEY("Flooring", "", cards=[
            ["Needs", "Non-slip (even when wet), easy to clean, hard-wearing, waterproof and not too cold or hard under the feet."],
            ["Choices", "Ceramic tiles with a rough finish, vinyl, or well-finished cement or terrazzo. Avoid polished or smooth floors that become slippery when wet."],
        ], note="Walls near the cooker and sink are best covered with washable tiles or paint. The worktop should be at a height that suits the cook to avoid backache."),
        EX("Worked example 1: choosing a layout", intro="A family of five has a small kitchen 2 m by 4 m with one door. The mother cooks alone. Suggest a layout and explain.", steps=[
            S("Fit the room", text="A single-wall or L-shaped layout. A U shape would be too crowded for 2 m width."),
            S("Choose", text="L-shaped: put the sink and cooker on the long wall and the refrigerator on the short wall."),
            S("Explain", text="This gives a short work triangle with little walking and leaves floor space; one cook means no through traffic problem."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: describing a good kitchen floor", intro="State three qualities of a good kitchen floor and give a reason for each.", steps=[
            S("Non-slip", text="Prevents falls when floors are wet or greasy."),
            S("Easy to clean", text="Spills and dirt can be wiped, so germs do not collect."),
            S("Durable and waterproof", text="Resists heavy use, hot pots and water without cracking."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Calling the work triangle 'the cooker, table and door'", "The three points are the sink, the cooker and the refrigerator (storage). The door is not one of them."),
        MISTAKE("Saying the galley kitchen has units on three walls", "Galley or parallel kitchens have two facing rows. Three walls is the U shape."),
        QUIZ("Which layout gives the best work triangle and the most worktop space?", ["Single-wall", "U-shaped", "Parallel", "None of these"], 1,
             "A U-shaped kitchen has units on three sides, so the triangle is short and storage is plentiful.", hint="Three walls.",
             feedback=["A single-wall layout has no true triangle.", "", "Parallel layouts are efficient but have traffic through.", "Layouts differ in efficiency, and one is best."]),
        QUIZ("Why must a kitchen be well ventilated?", ["To make the floor shine", "To remove heat, steam, smoke and fumes", "To keep food from drying", "To make washing easier"], 1,
             "Moving air carries away heat, steam, smoke and fumes.", hint="Think of what cooking releases into the air.",
             feedback=["Ventilation does not polish floors.", "", "Air movement can actually dry food; it is not the reason.", "Ventilation does not help washing directly."]),
        TIP("Exam link: layouts, work triangle, ventilation, lighting and flooring appear in Paper 1 and in Paper 2. You may be asked to sketch a kitchen plan and label sink, cooker and refrigerator. Practise drawing all four layouts."),
        PQ("(a) Name the three points of the work triangle. (b) Why should they be close together?", "(a) Sink, cooker and refrigerator. (b) To reduce walking and save time and effort, while leaving enough work surface between them."),
        PQ("Draw a plan of an L-shaped kitchen, and mark the sink, cooker and refrigerator.", "A sketch of two joined walls (L shape) with the sink and cooker on one arm and the refrigerator at the other end, with the three points forming a short triangle and the floor space inside the L labelled."),
        PQ("Give two advantages and one disadvantage of a parallel (galley) layout.", "Advantages: efficient work triangle; plenty of worktop in a narrow room. Disadvantage: if there are doors at both ends, people pass through the work area, causing disturbance and accidents."),
        PQ("State three qualities of good kitchen lighting or ventilation.", "Large windows or vents for cross-ventilation; extractor fan or hood over the cooker; bright, shadow-free lighting over work areas; natural light near the sink."),
        SUM("The work triangle joins sink, cooker and refrigerator; keep it compact and clear of traffic.",
            "Layouts: single-wall, L-shaped, U-shaped and parallel (galley), each suited to a different room.",
            "Ventilation removes heat, steam, smoke and fumes; good lighting prevents accidents.",
            "Floors should be non-slip, easy to clean, durable and waterproof."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 9. Cookers, refrigerators and appliances
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Cookers, refrigerators and appliances",
    title="Cookers, refrigerators, appliances and utensils",
    slug="fdn1-cookers-fridges-appliances",
    minutes=24,
    summary="Choose cookers, refrigerators and appliances by cost, fuel, size, safety and ease of cleaning, and use and care for them correctly. Know common utensils and how to keep them in good order.",
    objectives=[
        "Compare charcoal or wood stoves, kerosene, gas and electric cookers",
        "State what to consider when choosing and how to use and clean a refrigerator",
        "Name labour-saving appliances and utensils and their uses",
        "Describe how to maintain equipment so that it lasts and stays safe",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Cooker", "Gas cylinder", "Refrigerator", "Labour-saving appliance", "Utensil", "Non-stick", "Defrosting", "Energy efficiency"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Kitchen equipment helps us cook faster, safely and hygienically. A wise buyer does not simply choose the cheapest or the newest item. "
          "The right choice depends on the family's size and income, the fuel and electricity available, the space and the work to be done."),
        H("Cookers"),
        KEY("Common cookers in Cameroon", "", cards=[
            ["Wood or charcoal stove", "Cheap fuel in many areas. Smoky, slow to light, hard to control heat. Improved stoves with a chimney or metal body waste less fuel and make less smoke."],
            ["Kerosene stove", "Easy to move and heat quickly. Fuel can smell, spill and burn; keep wicks clean."],
            ["Gas cooker", "Very popular. Instant heat that is easy to control; clean. Needs a gas cylinder; leaks can cause fire or explosion."],
            ["Electric cooker", "Clean and safe with an oven, but expensive to buy and run and useless in a power cut."],
        ]),
        T("**Choosing a cooker.** Consider cost to buy and to run; the fuel available locally; family size; the number of burners and whether it has an oven; safety (stable, with a flame failure device if possible); ease of cleaning; and size for the space."),
        T("**Using and caring for a gas cooker.** Check hoses and joints for leaks with soapy water (bubbles show a leak). Never use a match to test for gas. Switch off the cylinder valve after cooking. Clean burners when cool and wipe spills at once. Keep the cylinder upright, outside or in a ventilated place."),
        H("Refrigerators"),
        D("A **refrigerator** keeps food cool, usually at about 1 to 5 degrees Celsius, to slow down the growth of bacteria. The freezer compartment is colder, at about minus 18 degrees Celsius, to store food for long periods.", title="Definition: refrigerator"),
        T("**Choosing a refrigerator.** Think about size (enough space for the family), price, running cost and energy rating, reliability and available service, whether the electricity supply is stable (some models suit solar power), and features such as adjustable shelves and a tight door seal."),
        T("**Using and caring for it.** Cool hot food before storing it. Cover food and keep raw meat and fish on the bottom shelf, below cooked food, so they cannot drip. Do not overfill the fridge. Open the door as little as possible. "
          "Defrost and clean regularly with warm water and a little bicarbonate of soda. Check that the door seal is tight. During a power cut, keep the door closed."),
        H("Labour-saving appliances and utensils"),
        KEY("Appliances", "", cards=[
            ["Blender (liquidiser)", "Makes soups, sauces, juices and purees quickly."],
            ["Food mixer", "Beats eggs and mixes cakes and dough."],
            ["Pressure cooker", "Cooks food faster using steam under pressure, saving fuel."],
            ["Electric kettle, toaster, microwave", "Heat water, toast bread or cook and reheat food quickly."],
            ["Mortar and pestle, grater, grinding stone", "Traditional tools that prepare pepper, melon seeds or cassava, still widely used and need no electricity."],
        ]),
        KEY("Utensils", "", cards=[
            ["Pans and pots", "Aluminium (light, cheap, conducts heat well), stainless steel (strong and easy to clean), enamel and cast iron, non-stick (needs care)."],
            ["Knives and boards", "Sharp knives and separate chopping boards for raw meat and for vegetables."],
            ["Others", "Wooden spoons, sieves, graters, measuring jugs, wooden or plastic ladles."],
        ]),
        T("**Care and maintenance.** Wash utensils soon after use in hot soapy water and dry them to prevent rust. Do not use metal spoons or scourers on non-stick pans. Keep knives sharp (blunt knives slip) and store them safely. "
          "Hang or stack pans neatly. Read the maker's instructions for electrical appliances, never put plugs or motors in water, and have faults repaired by a trained person."),
        EX("Worked example 1: choose a cooker", intro="A family in a town with an unreliable electricity supply wants a cooker. Which should it choose, and why?", steps=[
            S("Think", text="Electricity is unreliable, so an electric cooker is a poor choice."),
            S("Choose", text="A gas cooker (or a gas ring)."),
            S("Explain", text="Gas does not depend on the power supply, heats quickly and is easy to control. The family must check hoses for leaks and keep the cylinder safe."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: use of a refrigerator", intro="Explain why raw meat should be stored on the bottom shelf.", steps=[
            S("Problem", text="Raw meat may carry harmful bacteria and its juices may drip."),
            S("Bottom shelf", text="Any drips fall below, not onto cooked or ready-to-eat food."),
            S("Result", text="This prevents cross-contamination and reduces food poisoning."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Saying a refrigerator kills bacteria", "Cold only slows bacteria down. They start to grow again when food warms. Cooking is what kills them."),
        MISTAKE("Using a metal spoon in a non-stick pan", "It scratches the coating, which then flakes into the food. Use wooden or plastic spoons."),
        QUIZ("A gas leak is suspected. What should you do first?", ["Light a match to find it", "Switch off the cylinder and open windows", "Switch on the lights", "Cook faster"], 1,
             "Turn off the gas at the cylinder and ventilate. Do not use a flame or electric switch.", hint="No flames or sparks.",
             feedback=["A flame could ignite the gas.", "", "A switch can spark and ignite gas.", "Continuing to cook is dangerous."]),
        QUIZ("What is the main purpose of a refrigerator?", ["To kill bacteria", "To slow the growth of bacteria", "To cook food", "To add vitamins"], 1,
             "Low temperature slows bacterial growth, so food keeps longer.", hint="Cold does not kill germs.",
             feedback=["It does not kill them.", "", "It does not cook.", "It does not add nutrients."]),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's report says that the choice of a refrigerator and kitchen equipment is tested in Paper 2, and that some of these questions were least attempted or well answered. Learn a list of points for choosing and caring for each item."),
        PQ("State four points to consider when buying a refrigerator.", "Size to suit the family; price; running cost and energy efficiency; reliability and service; stable power supply; adjustable shelves; door seal."),
        PQ("Give three rules for the correct use of a refrigerator.", "Cool food before storing; cover food; raw meat on the bottom shelf; do not overfill; open the door as little as possible; defrost and clean regularly."),
        PQ("Name three labour-saving appliances and say what each does.", "Blender: liquidises soups and juices quickly. Food mixer: beats and mixes cake or dough. Pressure cooker: cooks quickly with less fuel."),
        PQ("How would you care for a non-stick frying pan?", "Use wooden or plastic spoons, do not use metal scourers, wash gently in warm soapy water, dry it, and do not overheat the empty pan."),
        SUM("Choose cookers and appliances by cost, available fuel, size, safety, ease of use and cleaning.",
            "Gas is popular; check hoses for leaks with soapy water and switch off the cylinder after cooking.",
            "A refrigerator slows bacteria at about 1 to 5 degrees Celsius; do not overfill, keep raw meat low and defrost regularly.",
            "Labour-saving appliances and utensils should be washed, dried, stored and used following the maker's advice."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 10. Hygiene
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Personal, food and kitchen hygiene",
    title="Personal, food and kitchen hygiene",
    slug="fdn1-hygiene",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Clean hands, clean food and a clean kitchen stop germs reaching our food. Learn the rules for handwashing, avoiding cross-contamination, safe storage and kitchen cleaning.",
    objectives=[
        "State the rules of personal hygiene for a food handler",
        "Explain how to keep food safe: separation, cooking, storage and temperature",
        "Describe how to keep the kitchen, equipment and waste clean",
        "Explain why each rule matters",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Hygiene", "Contamination", "Cross-contamination", "Food poisoning", "Handwashing", "Pests", "Refuse"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("A woman sells food at a roadside in Douala. If her hands are unwashed, if flies settle on the food, or if the cooked food is left in the sun, her customers may have stomach pains or diarrhoea. "
          "Good hygiene prevents this."),
        D("**Hygiene** is keeping ourselves, our food and our surroundings clean to stop the spread of germs and disease. **Contamination** is when harmful germs, dirt or chemicals get into food. **Food poisoning** is illness caused by eating contaminated food.",
          title="Definition: hygiene and contamination"),
        H("Personal hygiene"),
        T("A person who prepares food can pass germs to it from the hands, mouth, nose, hair and clothes. The main rules are:"),
        KEY("Rules for the food handler", "", cards=[
            ["Wash hands", "With soap and clean running water for about 20 seconds: before cooking, after the toilet, after handling raw meat or fish, after touching the face, hair or rubbish, and after coughing or sneezing."],
            ["Clean body and clothes", "Bathe daily and wear a clean apron or overall. Tie back or cover long hair. Keep fingernails short and clean. Remove rings and watches."],
            ["Cover cuts", "Cover any cut or sore with a clean waterproof dressing."],
            ["Illness", "Do not prepare food when you have diarrhoea, vomiting or a bad cold. Do not cough over food or taste with fingers."],
        ]),
        H("Food hygiene"),
        KEY("Keeping food safe", "", cards=[
            ["Buy and prepare", "Buy fresh food from clean places. Wash fruit and vegetables in clean water. Use safe (boiled or treated) water if the supply is doubtful."],
            ["Separate", "Keep raw meat, fish and eggs away from cooked or ready-to-eat food. Use separate boards and knives. This prevents **cross-contamination**."],
            ["Cook thoroughly", "Cook meat, fish and eggs until hot all through. Reheat leftovers until steaming hot, and only once."],
            ["Store", "Cover foods. Keep cold food cold and hot food hot. Bacteria grow fastest at warm temperatures, roughly between 5 and 60 degrees Celsius, so do not leave cooked food standing for more than about two hours."],
        ]),
        T("**Cross-contamination** is the spread of germs from one food (usually raw) to another by hands, knives, boards or drips. It is a common cause of food poisoning."),
        H("Kitchen hygiene"),
        T("A clean kitchen has no place for germs and pests to live."),
        KEY("Kitchen rules", "", cards=[
            ["Surfaces and floors", "Clean worktops, tables and floors after use with hot water and detergent. Wipe spills at once."],
            ["Washing up", "Scrape plates, wash in hot soapy water, rinse and let them dry on a rack. Use clean cloths and wash them often."],
            ["Refuse", "Put waste in a bin with a tight lid and empty it daily. Keep the bin area clean."],
            ["Pests", "Keep food covered and stored in closed containers. Block holes and keep drains clean to keep out flies, cockroaches and rats."],
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 1: handwashing", intro="List the steps for washing hands before cooking.", steps=[
            S("Wet", text="Wet the hands with clean running water."),
            S("Soap", text="Rub in soap, including between fingers, thumbs and under the nails, for about 20 seconds."),
            S("Rinse and dry", text="Rinse well and dry with a clean towel or air-dry. Do not wipe on a dirty apron."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: why separate raw and cooked foods?", intro="Explain why raw chicken should be handled apart from salad.", steps=[
            S("Problem", text="Raw chicken may carry bacteria such as salmonella."),
            S("Salad", text="Salad is eaten raw, so any bacteria on it will not be killed by heat."),
            S("Rule", text="Use separate boards and knives, wash hands in between, and store raw meat below other foods."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Wiping hands on the apron instead of washing", "An apron holds germs from earlier tasks. Always wash with soap and water and dry on a clean towel."),
        MISTAKE("Saying that heating food a second time many times is safe", "Reheat food once and until it is steaming hot all through. Repeated reheating gives bacteria more chances to grow."),
        QUIZ("Which action best prevents cross-contamination?", ["Using one board for raw chicken and salad", "Using separate boards for raw meat and ready-to-eat foods", "Rinsing the knife in cold water", "Leaving the chicken out to thaw all day"], 1,
             "Separate boards stop germs spreading from raw meat to foods that will not be cooked.", hint="Keep raw and cooked apart.",
             feedback=["The same board spreads germs from the chicken to the salad.", "", "Cold water does not remove bacteria.", "Leaving it out lets bacteria multiply."]),
        QUIZ("Which of these is a rule of personal hygiene for a food handler?", ["Wear rings while cooking", "Wash hands after using the toilet", "Taste food with fingers", "Cook with an open cut"], 1,
             "Handwashing after using the toilet and before cooking is essential.", hint="Think about hands.",
             feedback=["Rings trap germs.", "", "Fingers carry germs to food.", "An open cut can infect food, so cover it with a dressing."]),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's report says that questions on personal hygiene rules for preparing food were well attempted. Give clear separate points with a reason for each, for example 'cover long hair so that hairs do not fall into the food'."),
        PQ("State five rules of personal hygiene for a person who prepares food.", "Wash hands with soap before cooking and after the toilet; wear a clean apron; tie back or cover hair; keep nails short and clean; cover cuts with waterproof dressing; do not cook when ill."),
        PQ("Explain two ways of preventing food poisoning at home.", "Cook food thoroughly; keep raw and cooked foods apart; store food covered and cool; wash hands; do not leave cooked food standing for hours."),
        PQ("What is cross-contamination? Give one example.", "Spread of germs from one food, usually raw, to another by hands, boards, knives or drips. Example: cutting raw chicken then salad on the same board."),
        PQ("Describe how to keep a kitchen free of pests.", "Cover and store food in closed containers; empty bins daily and keep them covered; clean spills and crumbs; keep drains clean and block holes; use screens on windows."),
        SUM("Hygiene means keeping people, food and kitchens clean to stop germs and disease.",
            "Food handlers wash hands, wear clean clothes, cover hair and cuts and do not cook when ill.",
            "Keep raw and cooked foods apart, cook thoroughly, cover food and keep it cold or hot, not warm.",
            "Clean surfaces, wash up properly, empty bins daily and keep pests out."),
    ],
))

# ------------------------------------------------------------------ 11. Safety and first aid
LESSONS.append(lesson(
    module=M2, topic="Safety and first aid in the kitchen",
    title="Safety and first aid in the kitchen",
    slug="fdn1-safety-first-aid",
    minutes=22,
    summary="Most kitchen accidents (burns, scalds, cuts, falls, fires, electric shock) can be prevented. Learn the precautions and the first aid for burns, scalds and cuts, and when to seek a health worker.",
    objectives=[
        "Name common kitchen accidents and their causes",
        "State the safety precautions that prevent them",
        "Describe the first aid for burns, scalds and cuts",
        "Know when to get help from a health worker",
    ],
    key_concepts=["Accident", "Burn", "Scald", "First aid", "Fire safety", "Gas safety", "Dressing"],
    blocks=[
        H("Introduction"),
        T("Burns from hot water, hot oil and cooking fires are among the commonest kitchen accidents, and most of them can be prevented. The Board's report on this subject notes that kitchen safety is a popular topic, and teachers are advised to stress how to avoid kitchen accidents."),
        D("**First aid** is the immediate help given to an injured person before a trained health worker arrives. It is not a substitute for a doctor or nurse.", title="Definition: first aid"),
        H("Common accidents and how to prevent them"),
        KEY("Kitchen safety precautions", "", cards=[
            ["Burns and scalds", "A **burn** is injury from dry heat or flame; a **scald** is injury from hot liquid or steam. Turn pot handles inwards, keep children away from the cooker, use dry oven gloves, lift lids away from you, carry pots with both hands."],
            ["Cuts", "Use sharp knives (blunt ones slip), cut on a board away from your hand, carry knives pointing down, never leave knives in a washing-up bowl, wash and store them safely."],
            ["Falls", "Wipe up spills at once, keep floors dry and clear, use a firm stool not a chair to reach high shelves."],
            ["Fire", "Do not leave oil or cooking unattended; keep cloths and paper away from flames; know where a fire blanket or extinguisher is. Never use water on an oil or electrical fire."],
            ["Electricity", "Do not touch switches or plugs with wet hands; do not overload sockets; replace damaged cables; keep water away from appliances."],
            ["Gas", "Check hoses for leaks; turn off the cylinder after cooking; if you smell gas, open windows, switch off at the cylinder and do not use flames or switches."],
        ]),
        T("Also keep poisons such as kerosene, bleach and insecticides in labelled bottles out of the reach of children and never in drink bottles. Keep a first aid box in an easy-to-reach place."),
        H("First aid for burns and scalds"),
        T("The aim is to stop the burning, cool the skin and protect the wound from germs."),
        KEY("What to do", "", cards=[
            ["1. Stop and cool", "Move the person away from the heat. Hold the burn under **cool running water** for at least 10 minutes (up to 20 minutes is better)."],
            ["2. Remove tight items", "Gently take off rings, watches and clothes near the area before it swells. Do not pull off cloth that is stuck to the burn."],
            ["3. Cover", "Cover loosely with a clean, non-fluffy cloth or cling film so that the air and germs do not reach the burn."],
            ["4. Do not", "Do not burst blisters. Do not put butter, oil, toothpaste, powder or herbs on the burn. They trap heat and can cause infection."],
        ], note="Seek a health worker or go to a health centre quickly for any large or deep burn, burns on the face, hands, feet or genitals, burns in a baby or young child, electrical or chemical burns, or if the person is in great pain or shocked."),
        H("First aid for cuts"),
        KEY("What to do", "", cards=[
            ["1. Protect yourself", "Wash your hands if possible, and use a clean cloth or glove."],
            ["2. Stop bleeding", "Press firmly on the cut with a clean cloth and raise the injured part above the heart. Keep pressing until bleeding stops."],
            ["3. Clean", "Rinse the cut with clean running water and wipe around it. Do not remove anything deep in the wound."],
            ["4. Cover", "Cover with a clean dressing or plaster."],
        ], note="Go to a health centre if the cut is deep or long, if bleeding does not stop after about 10 minutes of firm pressure, if it is dirty or has an object in it, or if the person is not up to date with tetanus injections."),
        T("**Electric shock:** do not touch the person while the current is on. Switch off the power or pull out the plug first, then call for help. Seek a health worker even if the person seems well."),
        EX("Worked example 1: a scald", intro="A child spills hot water on her arm. Describe the first aid.", steps=[
            S("Cool", text="Hold the arm under cool running water for at least 10 minutes."),
            S("Remove", text="Gently remove any rings or tight sleeves."),
            S("Cover", text="Cover loosely with a clean, non-fluffy cloth; do not burst blisters or use butter or oil."),
            S("Get help", text="Because the person is a child and the area may be large, take her to a health worker."),
        ]),
        EX("Worked example 2: preventing accidents", intro="Suggest four rules for a safe kitchen when a toddler is in the house.", steps=[
            S("Cooker", text="Turn pot handles inwards and cook on back burners where possible."),
            S("Hot water", text="Keep hot kettles and pots out of reach."),
            S("Floor", text="Wipe spills at once and keep floors dry."),
            S("Poisons and knives", text="Store knives, kerosene and cleaners out of reach."),
        ]),
        MISTAKE("Putting butter, palm oil or toothpaste on a burn", "They trap heat in the skin and can bring infection. Use cool running water instead."),
        MISTAKE("Using water on a cooking-oil fire", "Water makes burning oil spatter and spread. Switch off the heat if safe and cover the pan with a lid or a damp cloth, or use an extinguisher made for fires of this type."),
        QUIZ("Which is the correct first aid for a minor burn?", ["Apply butter", "Hold under cool running water", "Burst any blisters", "Cover with fluffy cotton wool"], 1,
             "Cool running water for at least 10 minutes reduces damage and pain.", hint="Think of cooling, not covering.",
             feedback=["Butter traps heat and can cause infection.", "", "Bursting blisters opens the wound to infection.", "Fluffy cotton sticks to the burn."]),
        QUIZ("Why should pot handles be turned inwards?", ["To save space on the cooker", "So that nobody knocks them and spills hot contents", "To make them heat faster", "To cool the food"], 1,
             "A handle sticking out can be knocked or pulled, causing scalds.", hint="Think of a child passing.",
             feedback=["The purpose is safety, not saving space.", "", "It does not change heating.", "It does not cool the food."]),
        TIP("Exam link: the Board's report says that kitchen safety is a popular Paper 2 topic. Give causes, then precautions, then first aid. Always add 'seek a health worker for serious burns, deep cuts or if unsure'."),
        PQ("List five rules for avoiding accidents in the kitchen.", "Turn pot handles inwards; wipe spills at once; keep knives sharp and stored safely; do not use electrical appliances with wet hands; keep children away from the cooker; turn off the gas cylinder after use."),
        PQ("Describe the first aid treatment for a scald.", "Cool the area under running water for at least 10 minutes; remove rings and tight clothing; cover loosely with a clean non-fluffy cloth; do not burst blisters or apply grease; seek a health worker for a large or deep scald or for a child."),
        PQ("Describe the first aid for a bleeding finger.", "Press on the cut with a clean cloth and raise the finger; when bleeding stops, rinse with clean water and cover with a clean dressing; seek a health worker if the cut is deep or bleeding continues."),
        PQ("What should you do if you smell gas in the kitchen?", "Switch off the gas at the cylinder, open windows and doors, do not light flames or use switches, and leave until the smell is gone; have the leak checked by a trained person."),
        SUM("Most kitchen accidents (burns, scalds, cuts, falls, fires, electric shock) are prevented by simple precautions.",
            "Burns and scalds: cool under running water for at least 10 minutes, cover loosely, no butter or oil.",
            "Cuts: press with a clean cloth, raise, rinse, cover; deep or non-stopping bleeding needs a health worker.",
            "Seek a health worker for serious burns, deep cuts, burns on a child, and electric shock."),
    ],
))
